# Floating platforms over nothing.
#
# The point of this map is the two things a closed room cannot show:
#
#   `open` edges -- the sector simply ENDS, and walking off one drops you out
#   of the map. Every other map here is a sealed box where "no neighbour" means
#   "wall", which is the right default for a room and exactly wrong for a
#   platform.
#
#   `skirt N` -- the platform's edge, drawn N units below its floor, plus an
#   underside. Purely visual: nothing collides with a skirt. A floating sector
#   without one reads as a sheet of paper.
#
# Layout, looking down. Gaps are void.
#
#     y
#     56  . . . . . . . . [ 4 high ] . . . .
#     40  [   1 start   ]        [ 3 far  ]
#     24  . . . . . . . . [ 2 mid ] . . . .
#      8  [ 5 low, under the gap ]
#         0      20      40      60      80   x
#
# Sector 1 and 2 are one step apart, so you can walk between them where they
# touch; everything else needs a jump, and a missed jump is a fall.

# --- 1: the starting slab -----------------------------------------------
sector floor 6 ceil 60 tag 1 light 105 floortex PLATE1 thickness 2
  vertex  4  32   open tex EDGE1
  vertex 36  32   open tex EDGE1
  vertex 36  48   open tex EDGE1
  vertex  4  48   open tex EDGE1
end

# --- 2: a mid step, half a unit up, reachable on foot from 1 ------------
sector floor 6.4 ceil 60 tag 2 light 95 floortex PLATE2 thickness 2
  vertex 36  20   open tex EDGE1
  vertex 56  20   open tex EDGE1
  vertex 56  36   open tex EDGE1
  vertex 36  36   open tex EDGE1
end

# --- 3: across a gap, needs a jump --------------------------------------
sector floor 6 ceil 60 tag 3 light 110 floortex PLATE3 thickness 3
  vertex 62  32   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 84  32   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 84  48   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 62  48   open tex EDGE2
end

# --- 4: higher still, a double jump --------------------------------------
sector floor 11 ceil 60 tag 4 light 115 floortex PLATE3 thickness 2
  vertex 40  52   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 60  52   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 60  68   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 40  68   open tex EDGE2
end

# --- 5: a lower slab under the gap, to land on when you miss ------------
sector floor 0 ceil 60 tag 5 light 80 floortex PLATE2 thickness 4
  vertex  8   0   open tex EDGE1
  vertex 44   0   open tex EDGE1
  vertex 44  16   open tex EDGE1
  vertex  8  16   open tex EDGE1
end

spawn start  20 40 8
spawn mid    46 28 8
spawn far    72 40 8
spawn high   50 60 13
spawn low    26  8 2

# --- 6: an L, concave ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Deliberately not a box. A convex quad is the one shape where ear clipping
# cannot go wrong and where "is the point inside" has no interesting cases, so a
# map made only of boxes tests neither. This one has a reflex corner: the
# crossing-number test has to get the notch right, and a body walking the inside
# of the corner has two walls to resolve against at once.
sector floor 6 ceil 60 tag 6 light 100 floortex PLATE2 thickness 2
  vertex  -8  20   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  16  20   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  16  28   open tex EDGE2
  vertex   0  28   open tex EDGE2
  vertex   0  44   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  -8  44   open tex EDGE2
end

# --- 7: a plus, four reflex corners --------------------------------------
#
# Twelve vertices, four of them reflex. Ear clipping has to skip every reflex
# vertex and come back to it, and the fixed-point predicates have to agree about
# which is which on all three build targets -- a shape that triangulates one way
# natively and another under wasm would desync client from server.
sector floor 9 ceil 60 tag 7 light 112 floortex PLATE3 thickness 1.5
  vertex  70   4   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  78   4   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  78  12   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  86  12   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  86  20   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  78  20   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  78  28   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  70  28   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  70  20   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  62  20   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  62  12   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  70  12   open tex EDGE2
end

# --- 8: a long thin spur, with a sliver at the tip -----------------------
#
# Near-degenerate on purpose. The tip is a very shallow triangle, which is where
# an ear-clipping bug shows up first and where a normal computed from an almost
# zero-length edge would come out as noise.
sector floor 6 ceil 60 tag 8 light 90 floortex PLATE1 thickness 1
  vertex -30  36   open tex EDGE1
  vertex -12  38   open tex EDGE1
  vertex -30  40   open tex EDGE1
end

spawn ell    4 24 8
spawn plus  74 16 11

# --- 9: a hexagon -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every other sector in every map here is axis-aligned, and that is the one case
# push_out_of_sector's normals are EXACT for -- it takes the side's own normal
# precisely because an axis-aligned wall yields a clean normal and deriving one
# from the position instead leaks a little velocity between the axes on every
# tick of contact. A hexagon has no axis-aligned edge at all except two, so it
# exercises the case the optimisation does not cover.
#
# The coordinates are 12 * sqrt(3)/2 written to nine decimal places. mapc parses
# decimals as exact rationals rather than through a double, so what lands in the
# map is exactly the number written here -- irrational geometry is approximated
# by the AUTHOR, deliberately and visibly, never by the compiler behind their
# back.
sector floor 8 ceil 60 tag 9 light 108 floortex PLATE3 thickness 2
  vertex 28          78          open tex EDGE2
  vertex 22          88.392304845 open tex EDGE2
  vertex 10          88.392304845 open tex EDGE2
  vertex  4          78          open tex EDGE2
  vertex 10          67.607695155 open tex EDGE2
  vertex 22          67.607695155 open tex EDGE2
end

spawn hex   16 78 10

# --- 10: a tower ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every other sector here is a flat slab a couple of units thick, which makes
# this map a poor test of vertical geometry: nothing is tall enough to stand
# between the camera and anything else, so wall occlusion never arises and the
# lighting never has a tall surface to fall off across.
#
# A high floor with a deep thickness IS a tower: solid from 6 up to 26, with a
# platform on top. The skirt draws its sides, and they are what a body walks
# into and what a shadow falls on.
sector floor 26 ceil 60 tag 10 light 120 floortex PLATE3 thickness 20
  vertex 44  40   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 56  40   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 56  52   open tex EDGE2
  vertex 44  52   open tex EDGE2
end

spawn tower 50 46 28

# --- 11 and 12: a pillar standing INSIDE a hall --------------------------
#
# Nested footprints: sector 12's polygon lies entirely within sector 11's. That
# is the arrangement a pillar in a room, or a pit in a floor, actually is, and
# it works because geom::find_sector answers with the SMALLEST sector containing
# a point rather than the first one declared -- so on top of the pillar you are
# on the pillar, and beside it you are on the hall.
#
# Partial overlap is still rejected by mapc, and the difference is not
# pedantry: nested has an innermost sector to prefer and partial has none.
sector floor 4 ceil 60 tag 11 light 100 floortex PLATE1 thickness 3
  vertex  -40  56   open tex EDGE1
  vertex   -8  56   open tex EDGE1
  vertex   -8  88   open tex EDGE1
  vertex  -40  88   open tex EDGE1
end

# The pillar. Solid from 4 (the hall's floor) up to 12, with a top you can
# stand on -- so it blocks a body walking at it AND catches one landing on it.
sector floor 12 ceil 60 tag 12 light 130 floortex PLATE3 thickness 8
  vertex  -30  66   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  -18  66   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  -18  78   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  -30  78   open tex EDGE2
end

spawn hall   -34 62 6
spawn pillar -24 72 14

# --- 13 and 14: a bridge over a path -------------------------------------
#
# PARTIALLY overlapping footprints -- neither contains the other -- and legal
# because they are separated in height. The path's ceiling is 8 and the bridge's
# underside is 8, so nothing can be in both, and geom::find_sector_at decides
# which floor applies from where a body's feet are.
#
# This is the case "smallest sector wins" could not answer and had no business
# trying to: area has nothing to do with it.
sector floor 0 ceil 8 tag 13 light 70 floortex PLATE2 ceiltex PLATE1
  vertex  30  -20   open tex EDGE1
  vertex  86  -20   open tex EDGE1
  vertex  86   -8   open tex EDGE1
  vertex  30   -8   open tex EDGE1
end

sector floor 10 ceil 60 tag 14 light 125 floortex PLATE3 thickness 2
  vertex  52  -36   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  64  -36   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  64   -4   open tex EDGE2
  vertex  52   -4   open tex EDGE2
end

spawn path   36 -14 2
spawn bridge 58 -30 12

# --- lamps ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The sector light levels above are the AMBIENT FLOOR each platform is lit to,
# and they are deliberately low. These add on top of it with distance falloff
# and surface orientation, which is the difference between a scene that is one
# flat colour per sector and one where a slab's near edge is brighter than its
# far one.
#
# Warm over the walkable route, cold over the outliers, so the two read
# differently at a glance rather than only being differently placed.
# Two lamps over the starting slab, both OFF to one side. A lamp directly
# overhead casts its shadow exactly where the contact shadow already is, which
# hides the whole point of having both.
light 10 34 12  color 255 205 150  radius 26  intensity 1.6
light 31 46 11  color 190 205 255  radius 24  intensity 1.4
light 46 28 14  color 255 214 160  radius 22  intensity 1.55
light 72 40 15  color 255 226 180  radius 26  intensity 1.7
light 50 60 18  color 255 236 200  radius 24  intensity 1.55
light 26  8 10  color 150 190 255  radius 26  intensity 1.55
light  4 24 13  color 150 190 255  radius 24  intensity 1.4
light 74 16 18  color 255 236 200  radius 26  intensity 1.7
light 16 78 18  color 190 200 255  radius 30  intensity 1.8
light -16 38 12 color 150 190 255  radius 22  intensity 1.4

# On the tower, and low beside it, so its sides are lit across their height and
# it casts a long shadow rather than a puddle.
light 50 46 32  color 255 240 210  radius 30  intensity 1.8
light 40 36 10  color 255 190 140  radius 26  intensity 1.5

# Over the hall and its pillar.
light -24 72 20  color 255 230 190  radius 30  intensity 1.7
light -36 84 12  color 170 200 255  radius 24  intensity 1.4

# Under the bridge and over it, so the two levels read as separate places.
light 58 -14  6   color 255 180 130  radius 18  intensity 1.4
light 58 -30 16   color 200 215 255  radius 26  intensity 1.6
